  [PASS] 122-thm the analytic maximum (the root of u + 2e^-u = 2) agrees with a blind numerical maximisation of f to 1e-6 -- the theorem is not an algebra slip   (analytic u* = 1.59362426 f_max = 0.64761024; numerical u* = 1.59362426 f_max = 0.64761024)
  canonical  Sigma_M = a_0/(2 pi G) =  106.9;  Sigma_dagger = a_0/(pi G) =  213.7;  CEILING = f_max a_0/(pi G) =  138.4 Msun/pc^2 = 1.2952 Sigma_M
  alt        Sigma_M = a_0/(2 pi G) =  129.0;  Sigma_dagger = a_0/(pi G) =  258.0;  CEILING = f_max a_0/(pi G) =  167.1 Msun/pc^2 = 1.2952 Sigma_M
  [PASS] 122a THE ITEM AS POSED IS CORRECTED: Sigma_M = a_0/(2 pi G) is not the phantom's ceiling either.  The phantom is allowed to exceed it by exactly 1.2952, and the true ceiling is 0.64761 a_0/(pi G).  A phantom measured at 130 Msun/pc^2 would falsify the item's version of the bound and satisfy the framework's, so the distinction is not cosmetic   (ceiling/Sigma_M = 1.2952 on both footings; ceiling = 138.4 (canonical) / 167.1 (alt) Msun/pc^2 against Sigma_M = 106.9 / 129.0)
  the a_0-FREE corollary: at the radius where the excess acceleration peaks, g_obs/g_bar = nu(y*) = 1.2550 exactly, and
       the peak excess is g_obs - g_bar = 0.64761 a_0.  The ratio carries no footing, no distance and no size -- only Upsilon.
  kernel dependence, which is what makes 0.64761 a fingerprint rather than a generality: the 'simple' nu = 1/2 + sqrt(1/4 + 1/y)
       gives f -> 1 MONOTONICALLY (f = 0.9902 at y = 100, 1.000000 at y = 1e8), so its bound is a_0/(pi G), 1.54x higher,
       and is never attained at finite y.  Newtonian gravity (nu = 1) gives f = 0 identically: no phantom, no ceiling.
  [PASS] 122b the two kernels are cleanly separated by the SHAPE and not only by the number: Route A peaks at 0.648 at a finite acceleration and then DECLINES; the simple function rises monotonically to 1.0 and never turns over.  The existence of a maximum at finite g_bar is itself the falsifiable prediction   (Route A f_max = 0.6476 at y = 2.540, falling to 0.0425 at y = 50; simple nu gives 0.9808 at y = 50 and still rising)

