  mutation a_0 x 4.00: measured excess at y* = 0.224 +- 0.232 (mutated a_0 units) against the same predicted 0.648 -> -1.8 sigma
  mutation a_0 x 0.25: measured excess at y* = 2.362 +- 0.209 (mutated a_0 units) against the same predicted 0.648 -> +8.2 sigma
  [PASS] M122a mutation: the ceiling is a statement about a_0's VALUE and not an algebraic identity -- moving a_0 by a factor 4 drives the measured excess at y* to opposite sides of the predicted 0.648, and both moves are larger than the 122d discrepancy itself.  (A bug in the first version of this check: the bootstrap error must be recomputed IN THE MUTATED UNITS, or the significance is wrong by the same factor 4.)   (a_0 x 4 gives 0.224 +- 0.232 (-1.8 sigma), a_0/4 gives 2.362 +- 0.209 (+8.2 sigma), straddling 0.648)
  [PASS] M122a-power AGAINST INTEREST, the statistic's power is ONE-SIDED and that caps what item 122 can ever do: a_0 four times too SMALL is excluded at better than 3 sigma, but a_0 four times too LARGE is not, because that mutation moves the test bin to g_bar ~ 10 a_0 where the excess is a small difference of large numbers and the bootstrap error swells to match.  The ceiling bounds a_0 from below far better than from above   (a_0/4 excluded at 8.2 sigma; a_0 x 4 only at 1.8 sigma -- and the same asymmetry is why 122d itself is only 1.8 sigma despite a factor 1.9 in the central value)
  [PASS] M122b mutation: with nu = 1 the phantom vanishes identically -- no profile to fit, no ceiling to test.  The Burkert fit collapses onto the floor of its own prior instead of returning the Donato constant   (nu = 1 gives median fitted rho_0 = 1.00e-06 Msun/pc^3 (prior floor 1e-6) against the phantom's 0.0377)
  [PASS] M122c mutation: shuffling g_obs across the whole sample destroys the turnover -- the shuffled curve has no maximum near y* and runs strongly negative at high g_bar, so the shape in 122d belongs to the pairing and not to the binning   (shuffled binned excess runs -16.33 to +0.88 a_0 against the true curve's +0.15 to +1.24)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
self-audit against the five bug patterns
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  (1) TOTAL vs ENCLOSED: every mass here is ENCLOSED -- M_b(<r) = g_bar r^2/G point by point, never a catalogue total.  That is
      exactly why Sigma_ph collapses to a function of y alone and why the ceiling exists at all.
  (2) SPHERICAL on a DISC: caught twice.  Once in the setup, where the spherical-equivalent is legitimate because halo fitters use
      the same convention; and once in my own prediction of a hollow phantom centre, which is a sphere's behaviour and not a disc's
      (110f).  No projected surface density is quoted anywhere, because the true phantom of a disc is flattened.
  (3) an aperture on a SADDLE: not applicable -- one monotone radius per galaxy, no aperture, no local minimum.
  (4) covariance reshaped wrongly: no covariance matrix is used; errors are galaxy-level bootstraps.
  (5) a result that is really about UPSILON: check 122e IS that finding.  The excess at y* moves by more than its own predicted
      value across Upsilon 0.35-0.80, so item 122's data arm is an Upsilon measurement in a_0's clothes.  Item 110's r_0 and the
      rho_0 r_0 product are the robust half: the same-code column at fixed Upsilon shows how much of the offset is Li's freedom.

==============================================================================================================================
VERDICT -- items 110 and 122
==============================================================================================================================
  ITEM 110: MEDIANS WORK, WIDTHS AND THE TAIL DO NOT.  A Burkert fits the phantom's own rotation curve to 5% -- better than it fits
  the real one -- and the core radius, central density and their product all land within 0.1 dex of what Li+2020 recovered from the
  same galaxies, with no parameter anywhere.  The rho_0 r_0 product is thereby DERIVED rather than borrowed: item 5 read the Donato
  constant off other people's halo fits, and it now comes out of the phantom's own profile.  Against interest: the predicted
  distributions are much too narrow (110c), the phantom's rho_0-r_0 slope is not the -1 item 106 expects, the outer density falls as
  r^-2 and not r^-3 so no fitted halo can be right in the tail, and the surface-brightness diversity the analytic disc predicts is
  absent from the real sample (110g).  The shape is Burkert-like where the data are and Burkert-unlike where they are not.

  ITEM 122: A THEOREM, AND AN UNDERPOWERED TEST.  The framework does have an exact ceiling on the phantom, but it is NOT
  Sigma_M = a_0/(2 pi G) as the item said -- it is 1.2952 x that, 0.64761 a_0/(pi G) = 138.4 / 167.1 Msun/pc^2, reached at
  g_bar = 2.5396 a_0, with the footing-free corollary g_obs/g_bar = 1.2550 there.  Its EXISTENCE is already a discriminator: the
  simple interpolating function has no maximum at all, and no LambdaCDM halo has a universal one (2 rho_s r_s runs over a factor 14).
  The measured excess at y* is 1.9x the ceiling on canonical and 1.5x on alt, but only +1.8 and +1.4 sigma on a galaxy-level
  bootstrap -- high, not excluded.  Three things cap it and all are stated: the statistic swings by 3.8x its own predicted value
  over Upsilon 0.35-0.80 (bug pattern 5, and turned round it is an Upsilon meter returning 0.55-0.56); its power is ONE-SIDED, so
  it bounds a_0 from below at 7.8 sigma and from above at only 1.8; and the fitted-halo population does not pile up against the
  bound -- the median Burkert sits at two-thirds of it and a quarter to a third of the fits are above it.  NOT Kepler-grade.
  NO NEW LADDER RUNG COMES OUT OF THIS.  Inverted for a_0, the turnover's LOCATION gives 1.07e-10 [6.5e-11, 1.18e-10] and its
  HEIGHT gives 1.59e-10 -- but d log a_0 / d log Upsilon = -4.5, so the ladder's 0.05 dex would need Upsilon to 2.6%.  Recorded as
